= Scene 4: The Threshold

*Time budget: 15 minutes. Lesson: death, spot-revival, and Essence
Points --- the system's signature mechanic, demonstrated where nothing
else is trying to kill anyone.*

The old fey shrine stands in a clearing in the deep Weald: standing
stones overgrown with moss and creeping vine, humming with a resonance
felt in the teeth rather than heard.

#aloud[
  The standing stones are not a ruin. You can see it now, knowing what to
  look for: the moss traces lines that were carved long before the moss
  grew, and the resonance in the air is not faded power --- it is
  _contained_ power, held in check by the structure itself. At the centre
  of the stones, a circle of pale slabs is set flush with the earth,
  clean of moss, as if the ground itself refuses to grow over it.

  Something under your feet is very faintly vibrating.
]

- *Pâhâk (no roll):* Electroception picks up powered machinery below ---
  a steady electromagnetic murmur of magitek and moving fluid, perhaps
  forty feet down. Whatever is under this shrine is _running_. (This is
  also the moment Pâhâk's vague Perfector orders start making sense:
  someone is operating pre-fall apparatus out here.)
- *Kalariyar (no roll):* The stones know them. The resonance leans
  toward the Shade like a plant toward light. If Kalariyar steps into
  the circle, the vibration deepens --- welcome, or appetite.
- *Occultism D2:* The inscription spiralling the circle's rim confirms
  Sennakht's words: _The way is shut to the living. Give the door your
  death._

== Paying the Price

When a Deathless character stands inside the circle and offers themselves
--- a deliberate act, spoken or willed --- the threshold takes its
payment.

#aloud[
  The hum rises through your bones and becomes a grip. It is not violent
  and it is not cruel; it is a door asking, one final time, whether you
  mean it.

  Then it takes your death, all at once, the way dark takes a room when
  the lamp goes out.
]

*Mechanically:* the volunteer dies --- reduced to 0 HP, no roll, no
resistance. The circle of slabs sinks and grinds aside, revealing a
stairway descending into worked pale stone. Then run death and
spot-revival *exactly by the book*:

+ The character is dead. Their body lies in the circle.
+ At the start of their next turn (out of combat, simply "a few breaths
  later"), they may spend *5 EP* to revive --- returning at *full HP*,
  standing where they fell.
+ If they choose not to, or cannot, their essence departs for their soul
  anchor and reincorporates over days --- which, in a one-shot, means the
  character is out of the story. Nobody should choose this; make sure
  they understand the 5 EP option first.

#attention[
  *This is the teaching moment the adventure is built around.* Take five
  minutes and do it properly:

  - Before anyone volunteers, have the players check their EP (every
    pregen has 11). Spell out the trade: 5 EP buys you back from death,
    here and in the fight to come.
  - The revival is *full HP*. A character who limped out of Scene 2
    wounded gets a complete heal out of volunteering --- canny players
    should be allowed to notice this, and it is a fine piece of system
    mastery to reward.
  - Let the table feel the wrongness anyway. The volunteer dies. Mortals
    who watched it never quite forget it. Describe the death honestly and
    the revival gloriously --- a mangled stillness, then the body knitting
    itself back upright, ready for vengeance.
  - Connect it forward: "That resource you just spent? That is also your
    safety net downstairs." Now the stakes of the finale are legible.

  *Who volunteers* is a roleplay scene, not a die roll --- let them argue.
  If the party stalls: Kalariyar feels the door waiting for them
  specifically; Ushtâng has the most EP-per-sentiment reasons ("Vazhan
  paid more"); a badly wounded character has the pragmatic angle. And
  Sennakht's parting advice hangs over all of it: Šauhahten has paid this
  price _dozens of times_.
]

#note[
  *Rules corner cases, so you are not caught flat:* the death is a cost
  willingly paid, not an attack --- no resistance applies, and effects
  that trigger "when hit" do not trigger. Kho Prea's Gift of the Sun
  cannot buy the door off; there is no check to add successes to. The
  door does not damage the body --- dismemberment and body destruction
  are not in play here. And the seal is one-way: from the inside, the
  door opens freely for anyone (unless Sennakht was strong-armed and
  never said so).
]

== The Descent

The stairway opens into a vaulted corridor of pale stone, carved with
patterns that shift at the edge of vision. Šauhahten has hung alchemical
lanterns at intervals; their sickly yellow light makes everything look
jaundiced. From below: the sound of fluid moving through glass, and a
faint, rhythmic scratching --- pen on paper.

*Group check --- Subterfuge D3:* the party descends quietly. If most of
the party succeeds, Šauhahten is mid-work when they arrive: the
conversation below happens on the party's terms, and the party acts first
if (when) it comes to blows. If most fail, a homunculus spots them and
scurries ahead --- Šauhahten is standing, prepared, facing the entrance
when they enter, and the Chimera is already at his side rather than in
its alcove. The conversation still happens; he was always going to want
to explain himself.

#note[
  This one check replaces the whole "alert level" apparatus of a longer
  campaign version. It is a group check so all seven players roll
  something on the way in --- and so Pâhâk, several hundred kilos of
  Ironhands, gets to be the reason the plan goes loud. Let that be funny.
]

= Scene 5: The Laboratory

*Time budget: 65 minutes --- roughly 15 of talk and 50 of fight. Lesson:
powers and FP under pressure, and objectives beyond "kill everything."*

#flavor[
  The laboratory is beautiful in a way that makes your skin crawl.
  Glassware of extraordinary precision lines the walls. A distillation
  array --- copper, silver, and crystal --- dominates the central chamber,
  its tubes and vessels arranged with an artisan's care. Journals are
  stacked neatly on a writing desk. Small waxy figures, knee-high, move
  among the benches on quick precise errands. Something the size of a
  cart horse breathes wetly in an alcove, and the stone hisses faintly
  where it lies.

  And in a recess at the back of the room, sealed glass vessels glow with
  a pale, shifting light. Each holds a luminous substance that moves like
  something alive. Beside the recess stands a tall mirror in a silver
  frame, its surface too dark for the room it reflects.

  These are the souls. And this is the man.
]

== The Conversation

Šauhahten speaks before fighting. He has been waiting years for an
audience capable of understanding him.

#aloud[
  He is standing by the distillation array when you enter. Tall, even for
  a Mirror --- the fey shimmer of an Echo plays across his features,
  making his expression difficult to read. He does not reach for a
  weapon. He does not look afraid.

  "Ah," he says, as if you are guests who have arrived slightly ahead of
  schedule. "I had wondered when someone would come. Please --- do not
  touch anything. Some of these compounds are volatile, and I would hate
  for anyone to be hurt unnecessarily.

  "I assume you are here about the disappearances. I understand why that
  would concern people. I would like to explain, if you will let me. What
  I am doing here is important --- more important than you know, and
  certainly more important than the lives it has cost. I know how that
  sounds. I ask only that you hear me out before you decide."
]

#note[
  *His argument, in brief:* the cycle of death and rebirth is broken.
  Souls do not pass on --- they linger, they suffer, they are consumed by
  the Fog. Prima Materia is the key to restarting the cycle. Yes, the
  process requires the rendering down of souls. Yes, it is terrible. But
  the alternative --- an eternity of suffering for every mortal who dies
  --- is worse. He has done the calculus. A few dozen lives now, to save
  every soul that will ever exist.

  He is calm, articulate, and genuinely believes this. He is not lying
  and he is not performing. He is also wrong --- or at least, the voice
  in the Fog that guides his work has its own agenda, and _he does not
  know that_. If the party mentions Sennakht, something crosses his face
  that is almost homesickness: "She taught me to finish my proofs. I am
  finishing one."

  Let the party argue with him --- this is where seven players who have
  been fighting and rolling all evening get to just _talk_ --- but keep a
  hand on the clock. Ten minutes of debate is a gift; twenty is a
  problem. When you need the fight, he provides the exit: he asks,
  politely, whether they intend to destroy his work. Any honest answer
  starts the battle.
]

#attention[
  *The moral trap:* if anyone is tempted to agree with him --- and with a
  good table, someone will be --- draw their eyes to the sealed vessels.
  *Occultism D2:* the substance inside is not abstract distillate. Those
  are _people_ --- aware, trapped, suspended in a state of conscious
  dissolution. The extraction does not kill cleanly.

  The moment of temptation should feel real. Complicity should not be
  reachable --- if the party somehow sides with him anyway, Merzaghaal's
  scouts arrive within the hour and the session ends in a very different
  conversation. But that is a failure state to know about, not to plan
  for.
]

== Combat: The Proof Defended

*Encounter composition (7 characters):*
- *Šauhahten* (T1 Boss)
- *The Chimera* (T1 Elite) --- lurching from its alcove
- 4 *Alchemical Homunculi* (T1 Minion) --- snatching up vessels of
  something that steams

Stat blocks are in the Appendix.

#note[
  *Running the fight (7 players):* three enemy turns per round ---
  Šauhahten, the Chimera, all homunculi together --- plus Šauhahten's
  Legendary Reflex once per round after someone else's turn. Use the
  Reflex loudly ("as Zamazaha finishes her swing, he is simply
  _elsewhere_") so the players feel the boss fighting seven of them.

  *Šauhahten's priorities:*
  + *Protect the distillation array.* He bodies the line between the
    party and the array; whoever moves on it eats his focus fire.
  + *Distillation Overload early and often.* He knows they will wreck the
    array; he spends it while he has it.
  + *Fey Displacement* to stay out of melee. He is a ranged fighter with
    seven opponents; he never willingly ends a turn adjacent to Ushtâng
    or Zamazaha.
  + *Soul Siphon* whoever has the lowest Ward, to sustain.

  *The Chimera* guards Šauhahten's flank and punishes clumps with
  Sweeping Lash. Its regeneration stops at the first fire or Thermal
  damage --- Wishanya's flamethrower or Kho Prea's Sunfire Blessing.
  When a player figures that out, confirm it visibly: the wounds stop
  closing, and the Chimera _notices_.

  *The homunculi* screen for their maker and self-destruct gladly ---
  remind melee characters about Alembic Burst the first time one pops.

  *If a character dies:* this is the payoff of Scene 4. The player knows
  what death costs and what 5 EP buys. Give them the full ritual of it:
  the fall, the choice, the rise at full HP with a complete turn. Nothing
  in the session will teach the game's identity harder than dying in the
  boss fight and standing back up angry.
]

=== Destructible Targets

The victory condition is not killing Šauhahten --- it is destroying the
laboratory. Say this out loud when the fight starts, via Sennakht's
words if needed: _the man comes back; the work is what matters._

- *The distillation array* (central chamber): 15 HP, Armor 5. 2 AP and an
  *Athletics D3* (smashing) to deal 10 damage, or attack. When it goes,
  Šauhahten loses Distillation Overload and his composure cracks: +1d on
  his attacks, --1d on all his resist checks, for the rest of the fight.
- *The research journals* (writing desk): 3 AP to burn or shred. If the
  party promised them to Sennakht, this is the promise, breaking.
- *The soul vessels* (back recess): 2 AP per vessel; there are three.
  Released souls dissipate with an expression that might be gratitude.
  Šauhahten reacts with genuine anguish --- not for the people, for the
  material.
- *The reagent stockpile* (side chamber): 3 AP, and messy --- volatile
  compounds detonate in a *Burst 3* of chemical fire, 11 Thermal damage,
  *Dodge D2* negates, centred on the stockpile.

#note[
  Track destruction on a visible tally. The contract is fulfilled if the
  array and the stockpile are gone --- the rest is conscience, promises,
  and loot (each destroyed target reduces the salvage; see the Loot
  Table). As targets fall, Šauhahten's mask goes with them:

  - *0--1 destroyed:* composed, clinical, lecturing between attacks.
    "You do not understand what you are doing."
  - *2 destroyed:* the voice rises. "You are condemning _every soul that
    will ever die_ to an eternity in the Fog!"
  - *3--4 destroyed:* grandiose rage, tactics abandoned, the nearest
    enemy attacked. "I am the only one! The only one who sees! And you
    --- you _insects_ ---"
]

=== The Vanishing

Šauhahten does not die here. When he is reduced below 12 HP --- or when
the array and stockpile are gone and the fight is plainly lost --- he
breaks for the silvered mirror at the back of the laboratory.

#aloud[
  He looks at the wreck of his life's work, and then at you, and the rage
  drains out of his face all at once, which is worse.

  "Years," he says. "You have cost the world _years_. I hope you live
  long enough to understand what you did today."

  He steps backward into the silver mirror as if into a doorway. The
  surface takes him like still water takes a stone --- and then the
  mirror crazes from frame to frame and falls out of the world, and the
  laboratory is quiet.
]

#note[
  The escape is not an encounter to win; the mirror is keyed to him and
  shatters behind him. If a player has a held action or a clever
  interdiction, let them scar him on the way out --- a bolt through the
  shoulder as the silver closes --- but he goes. Award his full XP; the
  party beat him in every way that was on offer tonight.

  If the party somehow drops him to 0 HP before he reaches the mirror
  --- burst damage happens --- he dies mid-stride, shattering like a
  dropped reflection... and he is Deathless. His essence departs for a
  soul anchor that is very far from here. The epilogue barely changes;
  he simply returns angrier, later.
]

== Aftermath

The seal is one-way: from the inside, the door stands open for anyone
(if Sennakht never told them --- because she was Imposed upon --- give
them one bad minute at the foot of the stairs before someone notices the
threshold circle is inert from this side).

*Salvage:* see the Loot Table in the Appendix. In broad strokes ---
whatever the party did not blow up is worth money, the journals are worth
promises, and the soul vessels are worth nothing but mean everything.

= Epilogue: Contract Fulfilled

*Time budget: 10 minutes. Lesson: what playing on looks like.*

The party returns to Keshto. Merzaghaal pays the contract --- *700
#feathers* --- and listens to the full report without interrupting, which
from him is high praise.

Give each character one closing beat, a sentence apiece:

- *Ushtâng:* Vazhan's halberd, carried home. The Never-Still will bury
  the sergeant with it, unless Ushtâng would rather keep it.
- *Mbazân:* the watch dead are named and avenged; his own patrols sleep
  safer for what he learned here.
- *Kho Prea:* the Clear's thinning at the western edge remains ---
  whatever Šauhahten was doing, it was not the whole story. The Children
  of the Flame will want a full report.
- *Pâhâk:* the Perfectors are _very_ interested in the laboratory's
  intact magitek. The reply to Pâhâk's report is one line: "Observe the
  Mortefactors. Further orders follow."
- *Wishanya:* whatever she read of the journals --- or salvaged of them
  --- is a season of study. Methodical, as suspected. Predictable, as
  promised.
- *Kalariyar:* the door knew them. Sennakht, if they parted on good
  terms, offers one quiet thread to pull: "There are other doors,
  night-child. Bring me a rubbing of the next one you find."
- *Zamazaha:* seven hundred feathers, split seven ways, plus salvage.
  The name "Zamazaha" is worth slightly more in the Black Guard's
  ledgers tonight than it was this morning.

And for the table:

#aloud[
  Somewhere beyond the Weald, in the grey where the Fog thickens toward
  the old world, a tall figure walks with a slight new stoop, doing
  arithmetic. Materials. Years. Names.

  Something in the Fog walks with him, patient as weather, and says ---
  as it has always said --- _keep going._

  He lives to fight another day. So, for now, does everyone else.
]

#note[
  *Open threads, for whoever runs the next session:* Šauhahten is alive,
  educated by defeat, and guided by a patron whose nature this adventure
  deliberately leaves undefined --- it is not what he thinks it is, and
  it benefits from souls being rendered down. Sennakht knows more about
  the Deep Court doors than she has said. The journals, if they survived,
  are now somebody's problem. And the Never-Still remember who fulfilled
  the contract.
]
